\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren tree enumerates every primitive Pythagorean triple exactly once
from the root $(3,4,5)$ under three fixed integer matrices whose entries all lie
in $\{-2,-1,1,2,3\}$.  Reducing that tree modulo an integer $N$ produces a
multiplication-free, deterministic, non-repeating stream of residues, and thereby
a superficially attractive integer-factorisation heuristic: walk the tree and
test $\gcd(\text{hypotenuse},N)$ at each node.  We give a complete, unconditional
analysis of this ``hypotenuse dive'' and show that it fails for three independent
and individually decisive reasons.  First, \emph{trial-division equivalence}:
modulo a semiprime $N=pq$ the residues with nontrivial gcd number exactly
$N-1-\varphi(N)=p+q-2$, so the per-node hit density is
$(p+q-2)/pq \asymp 1/\pmin$; an exact closed form for the number of successful
$t$-node streams shows that any inspection schedule $S$ with $4|S|<p$ succeeds on
fewer than half of all streams, so constant success costs $\Omega(\pmin)$ nodes
and the scaling exponent is $\alpha=1$, matching the measured
$\alpha=1.007\pm0.088$.  Second, the \emph{guidance null}: the success count
depends on the inspection schedule only through its cardinality, so no ordering
rule, priority queue, traversal shape, or residue-class preference can change the
success probability at matched node budget.  Third, \emph{congruence blindness}:
every prime divisor of the hypotenuse of a primitive Pythagorean triple is
$\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, so the dive is identically blind on Blum integers
$N=pq$ with $p\equiv q\equiv 3\pmod 4$ and, when only $p\equiv 3\pmod 4$, reaches
only $p-1$ of the $p+q-2$ revealing residue classes.  Finally we prove an
unconditional separation from the collision paradigm: a distinct congruent pair
yields the factor $p$ exactly, and a birthday estimate gives at least $30\%$
success at $t=2\lceil\sqrt p\,\rceil$ nodes, so at matched budget the pair test
dominates every value-testing schedule.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Berggren tree; primitive Pythagorean triples; integer
factorisation; trial division; Blum integers; quadratic reciprocity; birthday
bound; null result.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The Berggren tree}

A \emph{Pythagorean triple} is a triple $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with
$a^2+b^2=c^2$; it is \emph{primitive} if the only common divisors of $a,b,c$ are
units.  Define
\[
B_1=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 & 2 \\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\ 2 & -2 & 3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_2=\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B_3=\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 2 & 2 \\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\ -2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix},
\]
each of which preserves the ternary quadratic form
\[
Q(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2-z^2 .
\]
Berggren's theorem (1934) states that the orbit map sending a finite word $u$ in
$B_1,B_2,B_3$ to $B_u(3,4,5)^{\mathsf T}$ is a bijection onto the set of
primitive Pythagorean triples under the customary normalisation.  Write
$\mathrm{node}(u)=(a_u,b_u,c_u)$ for the triple at address $u$ and call $c_u$ its
\emph{hypotenuse}.  Applying $B_1,B_2,B_3$ to the root yields $(5,12,13)$,
$(21,20,29)$, $(15,8,17)$.

Two features are of computational interest.  \emph{Multiplication-freeness}:
every matrix entry lies in $\{-2,-1,1,2,3\}$, so a child is computed from its
parent by additions, subtractions and doublings only, and reduction modulo $N$
costs at most a conditional subtraction per coordinate.
\emph{Collision-freeness}: distinct addresses give distinct triples, and
empirically the reduced stream inherits this, with no state repetition observed
within $200\,000$ nodes on any tested modulus.  Both are exactly what one wants
from a generator driving a gcd-based factoring search.

\subsection{The hypotenuse dive}

\begin{definition}[Dive]
Given a modulus $N$, a \emph{hypotenuse dive of budget $t$} traverses $t$ nodes of
the mod-$N$ Berggren tree, obtaining residues $c_1,\dots,c_t \bmod N$, and
computes $\gcd(c_i,N)$ for $i$ in an inspection schedule
$S \subseteq \{1,\dots,t\}$ chosen in advance by any rule whatsoever.  The dive
\emph{succeeds} if some inspected gcd is a nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{definition}

The proposal under test is that the structure of the tree might make this dive
behave like a structured random walk of cost $O(\sqrt{\pmin})$, as Pollard's rho
does, rather than like trial division at cost $O(\pmin)$.  Empirical measurement
over $200$ independent order-free dives returned a first-success node count
$v^\ast \approx 0.89\,\pmin$ and a fitted exponent $\alpha = 1.007 \pm 0.088$,
with constants roughly $11\times$ worse than plain trial division at matched
compute and with the ambient orbit under-sampling factor-revealing residues by
about a factor of $5$ relative to uniformly random Pythagorean points.  This
paper proves the theorems behind all three numbers.

\section{The congruence law: primitive hypotenuses are $1 \bmod 4$-smooth}

Throughout, $(a,b,c)$ satisfies $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and \emph{primitive} means that
every $d\in\ZZ$ dividing $a$, $b$ and $c$ is a unit.

\begin{lemma}[Odd hypotenuse]\label{lem:odd}
If $(a,b,c)$ is a primitive Pythagorean triple then $c$ is odd.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $c=2k$ and split on parities.  If $a=2s$, $b=2t$ then $2$ divides all
three, contradicting primitivity.  If exactly one leg is even then
$a^2+b^2 \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ while $c^2 \equiv 0 \pmod 4$, and rearranging exhibits
$4 \mid 1$.  If both legs are odd then $a^2+b^2 \equiv 2 \pmod 4$ while
$c^2 \equiv 0$, exhibiting $4 \mid 2$.  All cases are absurd.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:notleg}
Let $(a,b,c)$ be primitive and $p$ a prime with $p \mid c$.  Then $p \nmid a$ and
$p \nmid b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $p \mid a$ then $b^2 = c^2-a^2$ gives $p \mid b^2$, hence $p \mid b$ as $p$ is
prime; then $p$ divides $a$, $b$ and $c$, so primitivity forces $p$ to be a unit,
contradicting $p \ge 2$.  Swapping the legs, which preserves both the identity
and primitivity, gives the statement for $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hypotenuse congruence law]\label{thm:onemodfour}
Let $(a,b,c)$ be a primitive Pythagorean triple and $p$ a prime dividing $c$.
Then $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:odd}, $c$ is odd, so $p \neq 2$.  By Lemma~\ref{lem:notleg},
$p \nmid b$, so $b$ is invertible in $\FF_p$.  Reducing $a^2+b^2=c^2$ modulo $p$
and using $c \equiv 0$ gives $a^2+b^2\equiv 0$, hence
$(ab^{-1})^2 \equiv -1 \pmod p$.  Thus $-1$ is a quadratic residue modulo the odd
prime $p$, which by Euler's criterion happens if and only if $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$;
since $p$ is odd, $p \bmod 4 \in \{1,3\}$ and the criterion excludes $3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rem:aniso}
The content is that the binary form $x^2+y^2$ is \emph{anisotropic} modulo any
prime $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$: its only $\FF_p$-zero is $(0,0)$.  The Pythagorean
cone $Q=0$ intersected with the hyperplane $z \equiv 0$ therefore has no nonzero
$\FF_p$-points at such primes.  Nothing about triangles is used --- only the
definiteness of the restricted form.  This drives Conjecture~\ref{conj:main}.
\end{remark}

Every node of the Berggren tree is a primitive Pythagorean triple: the matrices
preserve $Q$ and preserve primitivity, and $(3,4,5)$ is primitive.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:node}
For every address $u$ and every prime $p \mid c_u$ we have $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:coprime}
If every prime factor of $N$ is $\equiv 3 \pmod 4$ then $\gcd(c_u,N)=1$ for every
$u$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A gcd exceeding $1$ has a prime factor $r$; then $r \mid c_u$ gives
$r \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:node} while $r \mid N$ gives
$r \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$N$ is a \emph{Blum integer} if $N=pq$ with $p,q$ prime and
$p \equiv q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  These are the moduli of Rabin encryption and of
the Blum--Blum--Shub generator.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Blum immunity]\label{thm:blum}
If $N$ is a Blum integer then $\gcd(c_u,N)=1$ for every address $u$, at every
depth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Any prime factor of $N=pq$ equals $p$ or $q$, both $\equiv 3 \pmod 4$; apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:coprime}.
\end{proof}

On a positive-density family of cryptographically standard moduli the dive is
therefore not merely slow: it transmits zero information, at any budget, for all
time.  (For instance $21=3\cdot 7$ is a Blum integer and the gcd is $1$ at every
node.)

\begin{theorem}[One bad prime suffices]\label{thm:onebad}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ prime and $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  Then $p \nmid c_u$ for
every $u$, and consequently $\gcd(c_u,N) \in \{1,q\}$ for all $u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$p \nmid c_u$ is Corollary~\ref{cor:node}.  Hence $g=\gcd(c_u,N)$ is coprime to
$p$ and divides $pq$, so $g \mid q$, and primality of $q$ gives $g \in\{1,q\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Sharpness]
The obstruction is congruence, not search depth: for $N = 65 = 5\cdot 13$ the dive
splits $N$ at depth two, since the node with address $B_3B_2$ has hypotenuse $85$
and $\gcd(85,65)=5$.  Moreover the law concerns the hypotenuse only: the child
$B_1(3,4,5)=(5,12,13)$ has a leg divisible by $3$, so a leg-based dive is not
structurally blind on Blum moduli --- though it remains trial-division-class by
Section~\ref{sec:count}, which applies to any value stream.
\end{example}

\section{Exact combinatorics of gcd dives}\label{sec:count}

We analyse dives in a finite model that is \emph{generous} to the heuristic:
values are drawn uniformly and independently from $\ZZ/N$, i.e.\ the tree is
granted perfect pseudorandomness.  Every negative result below therefore holds a
fortiori for the actual, structurally constrained stream.

\subsection{Revealing residues}

\begin{definition}
A residue $x$ \emph{reveals} a factor of $N$ if $1 < \gcd(x,N) < N$.  Put
$\Rev(N)=\{x<N : x \text{ reveals}\}$ and
$\Avo(N)=\{x<N : x \text{ does not reveal}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:avoid}
For $N \ge 2$, $\;\Avo(N)=\{0\}\cup\{x<N : \gcd(x,N)=1\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x$ does not reveal then $\gcd(x,N)\le 1$ or $\gcd(x,N)\ge N$.  In the first
case the gcd is nonzero (as $N \ge 2$), hence $1$.  In the second,
$\gcd(x,N)=N$ forces $N \mid x$ with $x<N$, i.e.\ $x=0$.  The converse is
immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hit count]\label{thm:hitcount}
For $N \ge 2$, $\;|\Rev(N)| = N - 1 - \varphi(N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|\Rev(N)|+|\Avo(N)|=N$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:avoid} gives
$|\Avo(N)|=1+\varphi(N)$ because $0$ is not coprime to $N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:semiprime}
If $N=pq$ with $p \neq q$ prime then $|\Rev(pq)| = p+q-2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\varphi(pq)=(p-1)(q-1)$ and $pq-1-(p-1)(q-1)=p+q-2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$\Rev(15)=\{3,5,6,9,10,12\}$, of size $6 = 3+5-2$.
\end{example}

The per-node hit density is therefore
\[
\delta(p,q)=\frac{p+q-2}{pq}=\frac1p+\frac1q-\frac{2}{pq} \;\asymp\; \frac{1}{\pmin},
\]
and everything below is a rigorous consequence of this single number.

\subsection{Streams, schedules, and the exact count}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{stream space} of budget $t$ is $\Omega_{N,t}=(\ZZ/N)^{\{1,\dots,t\}}$,
of cardinality $N^t$.  For $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,t\}$ the \emph{success set} is
\[
H(N,t,S)=\bigl\{ f \in \Omega_{N,t} : \exists\, i \in S,\ f(i) \in \Rev(N)\bigr\}.
\]
A schedule models an arbitrary guidance heuristic: any rule deciding in advance
which of the $t$ visited nodes to gcd-test, in any order.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact success count]\label{thm:exact}
For all $N,t$ and every $S$, writing $r=|\Rev(N)|$,
\[
|H(N,t,S)| = \bigl(N^{|S|} - |\Avo(N)|^{|S|}\bigr)\,N^{\,t-|S|}
           = \bigl(N^{|S|} - (N-r)^{|S|}\bigr)\,N^{\,t-|S|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The complement consists of the streams avoiding $\Rev(N)$ on $S$ and
unconstrained elsewhere; it is the product set $\prod_j A_j$ with $A_j=\Avo(N)$
for $j\in S$ and $A_j=\ZZ/N$ otherwise, of cardinality
$|\Avo(N)|^{|S|}N^{t-|S|}$.  Subtract from $N^t$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The guidance null}

\begin{theorem}[Guidance null, sharp form]\label{thm:null}
If $|S|=|T|$ then $|H(N,t,S)|=|H(N,t,T)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}: the right-hand side depends on $S$ only
through $|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order invariance]
For any permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,\dots,t\}$ and any stream $f$, the stream
$f\circ\sigma$ contains a revealing value if and only if $f$ does.
\end{theorem}

Three consequences deserve emphasis.  Selection rules are powerless: choosing
which nodes to test by any criterion --- residue class, nonzero-digit histogram,
learned score --- leaves the count unchanged at fixed $|S|$.  Traversal shape is
powerless: depth-first, breadth-first, best-first and random order are
statistically identical.  And scaling is exact: with $s=|S|$ one has
$|H(N,t,S)|=(N^{s}-(N-r)^{s})N^{t-s}$, so examining $s$ of $t$ nodes has exactly
the success rate of examining the first $s$.  Methodologically, any experiment
reporting an improvement at matched node budget is reporting an artefact.

\subsection{Union bound and trial-division scaling}

\begin{lemma}[Convexity estimate]\label{lem:convex}
For all naturals $b,d,s$: $\;(b+d)^{s+1} \le b^{s+1} + (s+1)\,d\,(b+d)^{s}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $s$; the step multiplies the hypothesis by $(b+d)$ and absorbs the
cross term using $b^{s+1}\le (b+d)^{s+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Union bound]\label{thm:union}
If $|S| \ge 1$ then $|H(N,t,S)| \le |S| \cdot r \cdot N^{\,t-1}$, where
$r=|\Rev(N)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $a=N-r$.  Lemma~\ref{lem:convex} with $b=a$, $d=r$, $s+1=|S|$ gives
$N^{|S|}\le a^{|S|}+|S|rN^{|S|-1}$; multiply by $N^{t-|S|}$ and combine with
Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trial-division scaling; $\alpha=1$]\label{thm:alpha}
Let $N=pq$ with $p \le q$ distinct primes and let $S$ be any schedule with
$4|S|<p$.  Then $2\,|H(N,t,S)| < N^t$: the dive succeeds on strictly fewer than
half of all value streams.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $S=\emptyset$ the success set is empty.  Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:union} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:semiprime} give $|H| \le s(p+q-2)N^{t-1}$ with $s=|S|$, so it
suffices that $2s(p+q-2)<pq$ whenever $4s<p\le q$.  Since $p-2<q$ we have
$p+q-2<2q$, hence $2s(p+q-2)<4sq<pq$.  Multiply by $N^{t-1}>0$ and use
$N\cdot N^{t-1}=N^{t}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Linear lower bound]\label{cor:linear}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:alpha}, if the dive succeeds on at least
half of all streams then $p \le 4|S|$.
\end{corollary}

The number of inspected nodes needed for constant success probability is thus
$\Theta(\pmin)$; writing the cost as $\pmin^{\alpha}$ gives $\alpha=1$, in
agreement with the measured $\alpha = 1.007\pm0.088$ and
$v^\ast \approx 0.89\,\pmin$.  A $\sqrt{\pmin}$ law is impossible in this model,
for any stream, guided or not.

\section{Coupling: quantified under-sampling of the tree}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:multiples}
For $q \ge 1$, the number of $x<pq$ with $q \mid x$ is exactly $p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Reachable revealing residues]\label{thm:reach}
Let $N=pq$ with $p \neq q$ prime.  The revealing residues not divisible by $p$
are exactly the nonzero multiples of $q$ below $N$, and there are $p-1$ of them.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x$ reveals and $p \nmid x$, then $\gcd(x,pq)>1$ has a prime factor dividing
both $x$ and $pq$; it cannot be $p$, so it is $q$, giving $q \mid x$; and
$x \neq 0$ since $\gcd(0,N)=N$ does not reveal.  Conversely if $0<x<pq$ and
$q \mid x$ then $q \mid \gcd(x,pq)$, so the gcd exceeds $1$; it is smaller than
$N$, else $N\mid x$ with $0<x<N$; and $p \nmid x$, else $pq \mid x$ by
coprimality.  Count with Lemma~\ref{lem:multiples} and remove $x=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Structural under-sampling]\label{thm:under}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ distinct primes and $p\equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  Then $p\nmid c_u$
for every address $u$, and the number of revealing classes the hypotenuse stream
can reach is $p-1$, strictly less than the total $p+q-2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first claim is Theorem~\ref{thm:onebad}.  For the second combine
Theorem~\ref{thm:reach} with Corollary~\ref{cor:semiprime} and note
$p-1<p+q-2$ since $q \ge 2$.
\end{proof}

Projecting the tree modulo $N$ therefore \emph{loses} hit density: the effective
density is at most $(p-1)/pq < (p+q-2)/pq$, a loss of roughly a factor of $2$ in
the balanced case $p \approx q$, on top of any further deficit from the orbit's
confinement to a small number of nonzero-digit histogram classes (about $99.75\%$
of the observed orbit occupies two such classes).  The measured $\approx 5\times$
under-sampling relative to uniform Pythagorean points is thus partly an exact
theorem and partly orbit concentration.  In the Blum case the loss is total.

\section{Unconditional separation: why the pair test dominates}

The rho paradigm tests differences, not values.

\begin{theorem}[A collision yields the factor exactly]\label{thm:collision}
Let $p,q$ be primes, $0 \le x<y<pq$, and suppose $x \equiv y \pmod p$.  Then
$\gcd(y-x,pq)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $x\equiv y \pmod p$ and $x<y$ we get $p \mid y-x$, hence $p \mid g:=\gcd(y-x,pq)$;
write $g=pk$.  Since $g \mid pq$ we get $k \mid q$, so $k=1$ or $k=q$.  If $k=q$
then $g=pq$ divides $y-x$, positive and less than $pq$ --- impossible.  Hence
$g=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
A stream $f$ of budget $t$ is a \emph{pair hit} modulo $p$ if there are indices
$i,j$ with $f(i)\neq f(j)$ and $f(i)\equiv f(j)\pmod p$.  Write
$H_\rho(p,q,t)$ for the set of pair hits in $\Omega_{pq,t}$.  By
Theorem~\ref{thm:collision}, membership in $H_\rho$ is genuinely a factorisation
event.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Injection bound]\label{lem:inj}
The streams whose values are pairwise distinct modulo $p$ number at most
$p^{\underline{t}}q^{t}$, where $p^{\underline t}=p(p-1)\cdots(p-t+1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Map $f \mapsto (\,i \mapsto f(i) \bmod p,\ i \mapsto \lfloor f(i)/p\rfloor\,)$.
The first component is an injection $\{1,\dots,t\}\hookrightarrow \ZZ/p$, the
second lands in $\{0,\dots,q-1\}$ since $f(i)<pq$, and division with remainder
makes the pair determine $f$.  Count the target.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Repeat bound]\label{lem:rep}
The streams that repeat a value number at most $(t^2-t)N^{t-1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For fixed $i \neq j$ the streams with $f(i)=f(j)$ number at most $N^{t-1}$; take
a union over the $t^2-t$ ordered pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Complement bound]\label{lem:comp}
The streams that are not pair hits number at most
$p^{\underline{t}}q^{t}+(t^2-t)N^{t-1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A non-hit either has all values distinct --- and then distinct values are
distinct modulo $p$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:inj} applies --- or repeats a value, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:rep} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Falling-factorial estimates]\label{lem:desc}
For all $p,a,b$: $p^{\underline{a+b}} \le p^{a}(p-a)^{b}$.  For all $p,d,k$:
$p^{k+1}+(k+1)dp^{k} \le (p+d)^{k+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Birthday inequality]\label{thm:birthday}
If $1 \le p \le m^2$ then $2\,p^{\underline{2m}} \le p^{2m}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:desc}, $p^{\underline{2m}}\le p^m(p-m)^m$.  Also
$(p-m)(p+m)\le p^2$, so $(p-m)^m(p+m)^m \le p^{2m}$.  The two-term binomial bound
with $d=m$ together with $p \le m^2$ gives $2p^m \le (p+m)^m$.  Chaining these
yields the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Birthday success bound]\label{thm:rhosuccess}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ prime, $p \ge 5$, and let $t=2m$ with $p \le m^2$ and
$t^2 \le q$.  Then $3N^{t} \le 10\,|H_\rho(p,q,t)|$: the pair test succeeds on at
least $30\%$ of all value streams.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:comp} the complement splits in two.  Theorem~\ref{thm:birthday}
bounds the first piece by half of $N^t$, since
$2p^{\underline t}q^t \le p^tq^t=N^t$.  The second obeys
$5(t^2-t)N^{t-1}\le N^t$, because $t^2-t\le q$ and $5 \le p$ give
$5(t^2-t)\le pq=N$.  Hence the complement is at most
$\tfrac12 N^t+\tfrac15 N^t=\tfrac{7}{10}N^t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rho dominates the dive at matched compute]\label{thm:dominates}
Let $N=pq$ with $p \neq q$ prime, $5 \le p \le q$, budget $t=2m$ with $p \le m^2$,
$t^2 \le q$ and $4t<p$.  Then simultaneously every value-testing schedule
$S \subseteq\{1,\dots,t\}$ satisfies $2|H(N,t,S)|<N^t$, while the pair test
satisfies $3N^t \le 10|H_\rho(p,q,t)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first is Theorem~\ref{thm:alpha} with $|S|\le t$ and $4t<p$; the second is
Theorem~\ref{thm:rhosuccess}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Concrete instance]
Take $N=101\cdot 487=49\,187$ and $t=22$, so $m=11$, $p=101\le 121$,
$t^2=484 \le 487$ and $4t=88<101$.  Every gcd-dive schedule succeeds on under
half of all streams, while the pair test succeeds on at least $30\%$.
\end{corollary}

The hypotheses are simultaneously satisfiable for arbitrarily large $p$: choose
$m \approx \sqrt p$ and $q \gtrsim 4p$.  Then $t \asymp \sqrt p$, whereas by
Corollary~\ref{cor:linear} a value dive needs $\Omega(p)$ inspections.  The
separation is quadratic and unconditional within the model.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What survives of the proposal}
Every attractive feature is real: multiplication-free expansion with coefficients
in $\{-2,-1,1,2,3\}$; no state repetition within $200\,000$ nodes on any tested
modulus; and confinement of about $99.75\%$ of the observed orbit to two
nonzero-digit histogram classes.  None of it converts into factoring power, and
the failure is structural: a better traversal, data structure, scoring function
or a hundredfold larger budget all leave the theorems untouched.

\subsection{The artefact behind the spurious significance}
Guided variants initially reported improvements at significance scores of
$z=12$--$24$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:null} says such improvements cannot exist at
matched node budget.  The resolution: a control that merely randomises the
visitation order --- introducing no guidance at all --- reproduced $z=21.8$
against the same baseline.  The effect was a pure traversal-shape artefact of an
unpaired comparison; under a correctly paired protocol every honest $|z|$ falls
below $2$, confirming the pre-registered null.  When an exact combinatorial
identity says a quantity depends on the budget alone, any measured dependence on
anything else localises a defect in the measurement.

\subsection{Constant factors and algorithmic content}
At density $\delta=(p+q-2)/pq$ the dive requires about $\pmin$ node expansions,
each costing several modular additions across three coordinates plus a gcd, while
trial division uses cheaper steps; the measured penalty was roughly $11\times$ at
matched compute.  Three algorithms are implicit: multiplication-free modular tree
expansion at $O(1)$ modular additions per child; the hypotenuse gcd dive at
$O(\log^2 N)$ per inspection and $\Theta(\pmin)$ inspections (never succeeding on
Blum moduli); and the pair test on the same stream at expected budget
$O(\sqrt{\pmin})$ for an unconstrained stream.  A subtlety: a stream that is
\emph{known} to be collision-free defeats a naive pair test on values, so the
collision-freeness advertised as a virtue of the tree is a liability in the rho
paradigm; what one needs is collisions of the reduced orbit modulo the unknown
prime.

\subsection{Scope}
The counting theorems assume uniform sampling, which is a hypothesis-free upper
bound on the real dive: the actual stream is deterministic and, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:onemodfour}, provably worse than uniform on a positive-density
set of moduli.  Conversely Theorem~\ref{thm:rhosuccess} is a statement about the
paradigm rather than about the particular tree stream.  The unconditional
statements --- the congruence law, Blum immunity, the exact hit counts, the
guidance null --- depend on no randomness assumption at all.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Quadratic-form obstruction to tree-based factoring]\label{conj:main}
Let $\Gamma \le \SO(Q,\ZZ)$ be a finitely generated group preserving an integral
ternary quadratic form $Q$ of signature $(2,1)$, acting on a $\Gamma$-orbit
$\mathcal{O}$ of primitive points, and let $\lambda$ be a nonzero linear
functional.  Then the set of primes dividing $\lambda(v)$ for some
$v \in \mathcal{O}$ is contained in a union of finitely many Chebotarev classes
determined by the splitting field of $Q$ restricted to $\ker\lambda$; in
particular it has density at most $1/2$ among all primes whenever
$Q|_{\ker\lambda}$ is definite.
\end{conjecture}

The Pythagorean case is $Q=x^2+y^2-z^2$ with $\lambda=z$, so
$Q|_{\ker\lambda}=x^2+y^2$ is definite and the admissible primes are exactly
those $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$: density $1/2$, with the bound attained.  A first test is
the binary case: for $\lambda$ with $Q|_{\ker\lambda}\cong ax^2+by^2$, show that
$p \mid \lambda(v)$ forces $\left(\frac{-ab}{p}\right)=1$, a direct generalisation
of Section~2.  If the conjecture holds, every ``factor by walking a hyperbolic
orbit tree'' proposal inherits an unconditional blind spot of density at least
$1/2$.  If it fails, a functional whose divisor primes are unrestricted would be
the first genuine candidate for a non-null tree dive --- though it would still
face the trial-division scaling and guidance nullity of Section~\ref{sec:count},
which are insensitive to the arithmetic of the stream.

Three further questions seem tractable: whether the leg stream, which carries no
congruence obstruction, reaches the full $p+q-2$ revealing classes at the correct
density (the theory predicts yes, with $\alpha=1$ still); a rigorous
equidistribution statement quantifying the $\approx 99.75\%$ concentration of the
reduced orbit; and whether the reduced Berggren orbit modulo the unknown prime
behaves like a random map, with the associated $\sqrt p$ cycle structure --- the
one remaining question not settled here.

\section{Conclusion}

The modular Berggren descent is a clean and genuinely elegant construction, and
as a factoring device it is null, for three independent reasons.  The revealing
residues modulo $pq$ number exactly $p+q-2$, so any schedule with $4|S|<p$
succeeds on fewer than half of all streams and constant success costs
$\Omega(\pmin)$ nodes: $\alpha=1$, matching the measured $1.007 \pm 0.088$.  The
success count depends on the schedule only through its cardinality, so guidance at
matched budget is provably impossible and every reported improvement is an
artefact of comparison.  And every prime divisor of a primitive hypotenuse is
$\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, so the dive is identically blind on Blum integers and reaches
at most $p-1$ of the $p+q-2$ revealing classes when $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  Against
these, a pair test on the same budget succeeds at least $30\%$ of the time at
$t \approx 2\sqrt p$ --- an unconditional quadratic separation in favour of the
collision paradigm.

